A||||6||Geoff Stephens||Thinkig about buying a Fiero.
Z||000000||Geoff Stephens||07-20-99||07:24 PM||||Hi...Im thinking about buying a Fiero for a daily driver.  Whats a good price to look for on these cars and what are some major complaints/ quirks that i might need to know about.  How about insurance too....im 25 and unmarried with a couple tix.  In comparison....i pay 170 a month for my 1999 Trans Am.  Im looking for dependibility, fun, manual tranny and the V6.  Thanks for the info! :)<p>Geoff
Z||000001||JetroGT||07-21-99||12:54 AM||||I pay about $720 for insurance every 6 mo. And as far as price you will be paying for a Fiero depends on the condition of the car. I personally like the 87 GT with the 5 spd getrag. The 88 cars are better but the parts seem to be very expensive for that year. When you test drive one that you might buy, drive it for at least 15 minutes or longer and get it up on the highway to look for any engin lights. As far as any quirks, all of these cars have some type of quirk or quirks. some common ones are the EGR valve, head light motors, and cracked exhaust manifolds. I can think of more but I have to get back to work.
Z||000002||ChadMan||07-21-99||08:24 AM||||The 88s are wonderful cars, with the redesigned suspension and bigger, vented brakes.  But they're well sought after, and suspension parts are starting to become expensive.  Expect to pay $4500-$6000 for a nice GT or $2500-$4000 for a Formula.  These prices are for cars without faded paint, worn interior, high miles, etc.<p>The 87s had the "old" suspension and brakes, but had the 5-sp tranny and redesigned headlight motors (quiet and reliable). Expect to pay $3000-$5000 for a nice GT.  The 87 SE V6 is somewhat rare, but a good buy - $2500 to $3500.<p>For bang-for-the-buck, you can't beat the 85-86 V6 cars.  In my opinion they have more electrical glitches than the later cars, and they had the 4-sp Muncie - a good strong tranny, but not as slick to shift as the 5-sp.  But they're fast, and they're cheap.  I've seen nice 85-86 SE V6s for as little as $1800.  The GTs are a little more, especially the 86 with the fastback bodywork.<p>And I'd budget $750 for a clutch, unless your chosen car has light pedal effort and smooth clutch engagement.  Good luck!
Z||000003||RICK||07-21-99||10:16 AM||||Ouch!  you guys are paying a lot for insurance, I'm from St. Louis, MO, my insurance company is insuring my 87 SE 2.8 as a classic car, full coverage for 1 year with a car value of 6000.00 is $125.00.<br>My insurance company said all the car has to be is over 10 years old and rare.  They will insure the car with it's value, so if you did a lot of upgrades (Which I have)they will insure it.<br>My insurance company is Shelter Insurance.<br>I hope this helps anybody.<br>P.S.  only liability insurance is $65.00 per year.<br>
Z||000004||yellowFormula||07-21-99||12:39 PM||||Well if I were you I would look for an 87 SE V-6. The fastback GT's (86-88) are way overpriced. Or even look for an 86 SE V-6 with a 5 speed tranny, they are nice too and cheaper/easier to find then 87 SE's.<p>good luck
Z||000005||Formula||07-21-99||01:56 PM||||I found my Formula at a dealership that couldn't sell it. I paid $1520 for it. It needs touched up here and there but its still a nice car to drive.  The motor has 70000 miles on it and the chasis has 130000 miles.  It has new brakes, struts, shocks, and tires. the only prob with it is when you first start it up it smokes a little bit, but after around 2 seconds it stops. i'm taking it to the pontiac dealership sometime to get the recall on the exhaust leak and the heat sheild fixed.
Z||000006||FieroMike||07-23-99||08:54 AM||||Geoff,<br>Hey! Are you over on LS1.com too?  Sounds like you are the same guy.  I'm "gmguy" always defending my little Fiero friends over here!!  Good luck finding a Fiero, I would say buy an '88 gt, the biggest complaints are the '4-speed trannies, so get a 5 speed.  Later.
